# Planur website — cache headers (Netlify / Cloudflare Pages syntax).
#
# The problem this solves: /assets/planur.css and planur.js are shared by every
# page and their filenames never change. Without this, a returning visitor can
# hold an old stylesheet against new markup — and because the class names change
# with the design, the result is not a slightly-stale page but a broken one
# (the nav collapses into a stack of plain links).
#
# Two halves, and both are needed:
#   · the HTML is never cached, so a visitor always gets the current markup and
#     the current ?v= stamp on the asset URLs;
#   · the assets are cached hard, because the ?v= stamp in the HTML is what
#     busts them. Bump that stamp in the pages whenever you edit planur.css or
#     planur.js — it is the only thing that forces a refetch.

/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Modest hardening. No CSP here: the pages carry inline <style> and <script>,
# and a policy loose enough to allow them would not be worth the false comfort.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN

# ── Tutor web app (/tutors/app/) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Flutter fingerprints most of its output but NOT these three. If a CDN caches
# them, tutors keep running an old build after a deploy — and a stale index.html
# can reference JS chunks that no longer exist, which looks like a broken app
# rather than an old one.
/tutors/app/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/tutors/app/flutter_bootstrap.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/tutors/app/flutter_service_worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# It is an application, not a page to be found in search.
/tutors/app/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
